 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "In MPS IIIB, a gene called NAGLU does not give the right instructions to make a working enzyme, which is a tiny helper that breaks down materials inside cells [Eaae4088, E4c1bac8, Ece19b4d]. Doctors call this a lysosomal enzyme deficiency, meaning the enzyme that should be working inside the lysosome, a small recycling bin inside each cell, is missing or very low [E8a7d8c7, Ee5d66d2, Eac469c9]. Because this enzyme is missing, a sugar-like substance called heparan sulfate cannot be broken down properly and builds up inside the lysosomes [Eae4e3d8, E1ed5a6b, E7a77f58, E4da5d49]. A paper reports that this buildup is seen together with loss of nerve cells and scarring of supporting brain cells throughout the brain and other organs [E2be3229, Ef988b3e]. A paper reports that problems also happen at the tiny connection points between nerve cells, called synapses [Ec820ac0]. A paper reports that the protective covering around nerve fibers, called myelin, can be affected too [E48729a8].",
   "citations": [
    "Eaae4088",
    "E4c1bac8",
