  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that are biologically similar",
   "text": "A paper reports that MPS IIIA, MPS IIIC, and MPS IIID all share the same heparan sulfate storage problem seen in MPS IIIB, even though each is caused by a different gene [Ec586fdd, E7373ae4, E9f300a9]. A paper reports that these same three diseases also share the lysosomal enzyme deficiency mechanism with MPS IIIB [E8282821, E108a4c3, Ef8f5811]. According to HPO, MPS IIIA, MPS IIIC, and MPS IIID share some of the same body signs with MPS IIIB, such as dense calvaria, meaning thicker than usual skull bone, coarse hair, and synophrys, meaning eyebrows that meet in the middle [E8ddb732, E92597bb, E4bf3fd5, Eb385fc2, Ec4ac373, E282b7e6, E98b729c, Efa494f5, E86de87e, E5dac422, Ed2941d0]. A paper reports that MPS II (Hunter syndrome) and MPS I (Hurler syndrome) also involve heparan sulfate storage and a missing lysosomal enzyme, although different genes are involved [E1f3fa46, Ea90608d, Ef5d5a50, E834d07e].",
